    "summary": "Original Aoxomoxoa mix with crisp sound; highlights Cosmic Charlie",
    "blurb": "The release features the original 1969 Aoxomoxoa studio mix, offering clearer dynamics than later remixes. Standout moments include “What’s Become of the Baby,” “Cosmic Charlie,” and a vivid intro to “China Cat Sunflower.” Sound quality is good overall with occasional pops at the end of the China Cat segment.",
    "review": "June 20, 1969 at the Fillmore East delivers an unusual listening experience: instead of a conventional live set, the available recording is the original 1969 Aoxomoxoa album mix. This version shines with a cleaner, more dynamic soundstage than the infamous 1971 remix, letting the band’s early psychedelic textures breathe. Highlights flow naturally – “What’s Become of the Baby” opens with bright guitars, “Cosmic Charlie” rockets into spacey organ swells, and the brief “China Cat Sunflower” intro teases the classic melody before a few audible pops cut in at its tail. While the setlist played that night featured blues‑rock staples like “I'm A King Bee,” “Dire Wolf,” and “Alligator,” those performances are not captured here; listeners get an excellent studio snapshot of the era’s material instead.",
